    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> was created on 14 August 1947 by the partition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>, in riots that killed between 200,000 and 2,000,000 people and the largest migration in history; it adopted a republican constitution in 1956 and became an Islamic republic of two provinces, East and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-pakistan">West Pakistan</a>.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-pakistan">East Pakistan</a> seceded as Bangladesh in 1971 after a nine-month war in which independent researchers estimate 300,000 to 500,000 civilians died; the loss drove <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> to build the nuclear weapons it tested in 1998, the seventh state to do so.</li><li>Military coups in 1958, 1977 and 1999 put generals in power for three long stretches, and the first transfer from one elected government to another came only in May 2013.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> was coined in January 1933 by the activist Choudhry Rahmat Ali in a pamphlet, Now or Never, as an acronym of Panjab, Afghania, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balochistan">Baluchistan</a>; in Persian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a> it also means 'the land of the pure'.</p></aside>
    </section>

