eral Zia-ul-Haq becomes president in 1978 and Islamises law and the economy.</title></g><g class="rf-ev"><line x1="458.3" x2="458.3" y1="160" y2="166"/><circle cx="458.3" cy="160" r="8"/><text x="458.3" y="164" text-anchor="middle">5</text><title>1998: Nawaz Sharif authorises nuclear tests in answer to India's, making Pakistan the seventh nuclear-armed state.</title></g><g class="rf-ev"><line x1="468.7" x2="468.7" y1="160" y2="166"/><circle cx="468.7" cy="160" r="8"/><text x="468.7" y="164" text-anchor="middle">6</text><title>1999: The Kargil War brings Pakistan and India to the brink, and General Pervez Musharraf takes power in a bloodless coup.</title></g><g class="rf-ev"><line x1="562.6" x2="562.6" y1="160" y2="166"/><circle cx="562.6" cy="160" r="8"/><text x="562.6" y="164" text-anchor="middle">7</text><title>2008: After Benazir Bhutto's assassination in 2007 the Pakistan Peoples Party wins the election; Musharraf resigns on 18 August and Asif Ali Zardari succeeds him.</title></g><g class="rf-ev"><line x1="614.8" x2="614.8" y1="160" y2="166"/><circle cx="614.8" cy="160" r="8"/><text x="614.8" y="164" text-anchor="middle">8</text><title>2013: The Pakistan Muslim League (N) wins the general election and Nawaz Sharif becomes prime minister for the third time, in the first successful democratic transition.</title></g><text class="rf-axis" x="20" y="200">1956 CE</text><text class="rf-axis" x="740" y="200" text-anchor="end">2025 CE</text></svg><figcaption>Relative power on the Institute's 0–10 scale (each profile scaled to its own peak). Numbers mark the key events below.</figcaption></figure><ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1956</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> adopts a republican constitution and becomes an Islamic republic with two geographically separate provinces, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-pakistan">East Pakistan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-pakistan">West Pakistan</a>.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1958</span><span class="rf-text">President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iskander-mirza">Iskander Mirza</a> imposes martial law and is succeeded by General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayub-khan">Ayub Khan</a>, who suspends the constitution; a presidential constitution follows in 1962.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1971</span><span class="rf-text">After the military refuses to transfer power to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awami-league">Awami League</a> and launches Operation Searchlight, war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> ends in defeat and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-pakistan">East Pakistan</a> becomes Bangladesh.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1977</span><span class="rf-text">A military coup against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulfikar-ali-bhutto">Zulfikar Ali Bhutto</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> Peoples Party ends democracy; General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-zia-ul-haq">Zia-ul-Haq</a> becomes president in 1978 and Islamises law and the economy.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1998</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nawaz-sharif">Nawaz Sharif</a> authorises nuclear tests in answer to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s, making <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> the seventh nuclear-armed state.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1999</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kargil-war">Kargil War</a> brings <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> to the brink, and General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pervez-musharraf">Pervez Musharraf</a> takes power in a bloodless coup.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2008</span><span class="rf-text">After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benazir-bhutto">Benazir Bhutto</a>'s assassination in 2007 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> Peoples Party wins the election; Musharraf resigns on 18 August and Asif Ali Zardari succeeds him.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2013</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> Muslim League (N) wins the general election and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nawaz-sharif">Nawaz Sharif</a> becomes prime minister for the third time, in the first successful democratic transition.</span></li></ol>
