    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-jinnah"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-jinnah.jpg" alt="Muhammad Ali Jinnah" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-jinnah">Muhammad Ali Jinnah</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jinnah1945c.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> plain held Mehrgarh, the cities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley Civilisation</a> and Gandhara, and official chronology dates the foundation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-qasim">Muhammad ibn Qasim</a>'s conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> in 711. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karachi">Karachi</a> in 1839 and Sindh in 1843 and by 1893 ruled the whole region. Sir Syed Ahmed Khan's two-nation theory led to the All-India Muslim League in 1906, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-jinnah">Muhammad Ali Jinnah</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore-resolution">Lahore Resolution</a> of 1940 demanded a separate state. The League won 90 percent of the Muslim seats in 1946, and on 3 June 1947 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-mountbatten">Lord Mountbatten</a> announced partition. Pakistan was established on 14 August 1947 from the Muslim-majority provinces of the north-west and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-bengal">East Bengal</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> riots between 200,000 and 2,000,000 people were killed; some 6.5 million Muslims moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-pakistan">West Pakistan</a> and 4.7 million <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, the largest migration in human history, and a dispute over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jammu">Jammu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> brought war with India in 1947–1948.</p><p>Jinnah became the first governor-general and died of tuberculosis on 11 September 1948; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaquat-ali-khan">Liaquat Ali Khan</a> was the first prime minister. From 1947 to 1956 Pakistan was a monarchy within the Commonwealth under two monarchs. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/objectives-resolution">Objectives Resolution</a> of March 1949 affirmed that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> over the universe belongs to God alone, and it became the preamble of the constitutions of 1956, 1962 and 1973. The constitution of 1956 made Pakistan an Islamic republic of two provinces, East and West Pakistan, separated by the breadth of India.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/cotton"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cotton.jpg" alt="Cotton" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/cotton">Cotton</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cotton_-_%E0%B4%AA%E0%B4%B0%E0%B5%81%E0%B4%A4%E0%B5%8D%E0%B4%A4%E0%B4%BF_03.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">കാക്കര, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> is the fifth most populous country, with more than 241.5 million people in 2023 and the second-largest Muslim population in the world; 96.35 percent are Muslim, 2.17 percent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> and 1.37 percent Christian. By the 2023 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> Punjabis are 36.98 percent, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pashtuns">Pashtuns</a> 18.15 and Sindhis 14.31 of a people speaking 75 to 85 languages, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a> and English official. A diaspora of seven to ten million sent home $38.3 billion in 2024–25. Literacy was 62.3 percent in 2018, with wide regional and gender gaps, and the state spends 2.3 percent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gross-domestic-product">GDP</a> on education, among the lowest in the region; about one in six girls is married in childhood.</p><p>The economy ranks 26th in the world by purchasing power and 42nd by nominal GDP, $450 billion in 2026, or $1,840 a head. Agriculture employs 43.5 percent of the labour force: Pakistan is the seventh wheat producer, fourth in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and milk and fifth in sugar. Textiles contribute 9.5 percent of GDP and employ some 15 million; services make 58.42 percent of GDP, and ICT exports reached a record $2.6 billion in 2023–24 with three million freelancers. Beneath lie the fifth-largest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> reserves in the world and the Reko Diq <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and gold deposit. Yet 44.7 percent of the population live below $4.20 a day, growth of 2.5 percent in 2024 was the second lowest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a>, women's economic participation ranks near the bottom of the world and the trade deficit was $21.3 billion in 2023.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> has the world's seventh-largest standing armed forces, about 660,000 active troops and 291,000 paramilitary personnel in 2024, in an Army, Navy and Air Force that have wielded significant influence over politics since 1947. The National Command Authority controls the nuclear arsenal under a doctrine of full-spectrum deterrence. The nuclear programme began in 1972 after the loss of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-pakistan">East Pakistan</a>, Abdul Qadeer Khan founded the Kahuta Research Laboratories in 1976 to enrich uranium, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s tests in May 1998 were answered with Pakistan's own, making it the seventh nuclear-armed state. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> supply its equipment.</p><p>Four conventional wars have been fought with India: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> war of 1947–1948, which left Pakistan a third of the region; 1965; 1971, when India's support for the Bengali Mukti Bahini ended in Indian victory and the independence of Bangladesh; and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kargil-war">Kargil War</a> of 1999. The Inter-Services Intelligence, founded within a year of independence, channelled American resources to the Afghan mujahideen against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and later helped establish the Taliban. Since 2001 the army has fought the Tehrik-e-Taliban Pakistan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-west-frontier-province">Khyber Pakhtunkhwa</a>; by its own reckoning the war on terrorism has cost up to $118 billion, over 81,000 casualties and 1.8 million displaced civilians. In September 2025 a Strategic Mutual Defence Agreement extended Pakistan's nuclear umbrella to Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/zulfikar-ali-bhutto"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zulfikar-ali-bhutto.jpg" alt="Zulfikar Ali Bhutto" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/zulfikar-ali-bhutto">Zulfikar Ali Bhutto</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Z_A_Bhutto_(President_of_Pakistan).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown photographer. Office of President Zulfikar Ali Bhutto., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> is a federal parliamentary republic with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> as the state religion. Its constitutions of 1956 and 1962 were each swept away by generals, and the 1973 constitution written under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulfikar-ali-bhutto">Zulfikar Ali Bhutto</a>, which declared an Islamic Republic whose laws must conform to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> and Sunnah, was suspended by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-zia-ul-haq">Zia-ul-Haq</a>'s martial law in 1977 and restored with amendments in 1985. Military rulers governed de facto as presidents in 1958–1971, 1977–1988 and 1999–2008; Zia instituted Shariat courts, and the Ahmadis have been barred from calling themselves Muslims since 1974.</p><p>Today the president is a ceremonial head of state chosen by an electoral college. The National Assembly has 336 members, 70 seats reserved for women and religious minorities, and the 96-member Senate gives the four provinces, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-west-frontier-province">Khyber Pakhtunkhwa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balochistan">Balochistan</a>, equal weight. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benazir-bhutto">Benazir Bhutto</a> became the first woman prime minister in 1988, and power alternated between her Pakistan Peoples Party and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nawaz-sharif">Nawaz Sharif</a>'s Pakistan Muslim League (N) until Musharraf's coup; the first democratic transition came in May 2013, Imran Khan's PTI won in 2018, and Shehbaz Sharif took office after a no-confidence vote in 2022 and again in 2024. All sex outside marriage is a crime, male homosexuality carries up to ten years, hundreds of women die each year in honour killings, and Reporters Without Borders ranked the press 158th of 180 in 2025.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> is a middle power and a declared nuclear-weapon state, a member of the Organisation of Islamic Cooperation and the Shanghai Cooperation Organisation and a major non-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> ally of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> since 2004. Its founders sought to lead the Muslim world, and though their 'Islamistan' found no takers, the OIC they wanted was formed in the 1970s; Pakistan backed independence movements from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> and championed the Palestinian cause. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> calls it an 'Iron Brother': China is its largest trading partner and the builder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gwadar">Gwadar</a> port and the China–Pakistan Economic Corridor. Relations with Washington have been 'on-and-off', from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> alliance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanctions">sanctions</a> over the nuclear programme and mistrust during the twenty-year war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> divides the subcontinent: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> holds about 45.1 percent, Pakistan 38.2 and China 20, along a Line of Control drawn in 1949, and three of the four Indo-Pakistani wars were fought over it. Pakistan sheltered millions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a> after the Soviet invasion, sustained the mujahideen and the Taliban, and ordered undocumented Afghans expelled in October 2023. In culture the national poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-iqbal">Muhammad Iqbal</a> shaped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a> letters, Abdus Salam won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Physics and Malala Yousafzai became the youngest Nobel laureate; Pakistan launched the first satellite of any Muslim nation in 1990 and has won every major ICC cricket tournament.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Into the Present</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>'s politics since 2008 have been civilian but unsteady. Musharraf resigned under threat of impeachment on 18 August 2008; the Pakistan Peoples Party governed until the Pakistan Muslim League (N) won in 2013 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nawaz-sharif">Nawaz Sharif</a> returned. Imran Khan's Pakistan Tehreek-e-Insaf won in 2018 and lost a no-confidence vote in April 2022; in the election of 2024 PTI-backed independents formed the largest bloc, yet Shehbaz Sharif was elected prime minister by a coalition of the PML (N) and PPP. The military's influence persists, and Freedom House rated internet freedom 'Not Free' in 2024.</p><p>The economy is the pressing weakness. Growth of 2.5 percent in 2024 was the second lowest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a>, 44.7 percent of Pakistanis live below the international poverty line, 16.5 percent in extreme poverty, and the trade deficit stood at $21.3 billion in 2023; the country scored 27 of 100 on the Corruption Perceptions Index in 2024 and ranks near the bottom for women's economic participation. Terrorism continues in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-west-frontier-province">Khyber Pakhtunkhwa</a>, where the army has fought the Tehrik-e-Taliban since 2001. Against this stand remittances of $38.3 billion, ICT exports and freelancers earning nearly $1 billion in foreign exchange, nuclear and hydroelectric power, the untapped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> and the Reko Diq mine, and new weight abroad: the Strategic Mutual Defence Agreement with Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> of September 2025 makes an attack on either an attack on both.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Islamic Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, proclaimed in 1956 in the state carved from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> in 1947, lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-pakistan">East Pakistan</a> as Bangladesh in 1971, answered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> with nuclear weapons in 1998 and has swung between generals and elected governments, a middle power of 241 million people tied to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and still facing poverty, terrorism and the dispute over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>.</em></p>

